    "thread_id": "thread_eaeb6f5ed3ff",
    "query": "Can I claim Section 80D deduction without health checkup?",
    "financial_year": "2024-25",
    "draft_answer": "**Short answer:**  \nYes. Under the **Old Tax Regime** you may claim a deduction under **Section 80D** for health‑insurance premiums even if you do **not** incur any preventive health‑check‑up expense. The preventive‑check‑up component is **optional** and, if claimed, is limited to a maximum of ₹5,000.  \n\n---\n\n### Legal basis (FY 2024‑25)\n\n| Provision | What it says | Relevance |\n|-----------|--------------|-----------|\n| **C1 – ITR‑3 Validation Rules (AY 2025‑26)** | “Deduction of Preventive Health Check‑up under section 80D … cannot be greater than ₹5,000. Deduction is available only if ‘Old Tax Regime’ is selected.” | Sets the *upper limit* for the preventive‑check‑up amount; it does **not** make the check‑up mandatory. |\n| **C2 – ITR‑4 Validation Rules (AY 2024‑25)** | “If Old Tax Regime is selected, … the amount of preventive health check‑up of all the fields combined together should not exceed ₹5,000.” | Reinforces that the check‑up amount is a *cap*, not a prerequisite. |\n| **C5 – ITR‑4 Validation Rules (AY 2025‑26)** | “If Old Tax Regime is selected, then … the amount of preventive health check‑up … should not exceed ₹5,000.” | Same limitation reiterated for the next assessment year. |\n| **C6 – ITR‑4 Validation Rules (AY 2024‑25)** – dropdown logic | “In Schedule 80D, Deduction at sl.no.1a ‘Self and Family’ can be claimed only if dropdown at sl.no.1 is selected as ‘No’ (i.e., not claiming preventive health‑check‑up).” | Shows that the software allows you to **opt‑out** of the preventive‑check‑up component while still claiming the insurance premium. |\n| **C1 – Schedule‑blank rule for New Regime** | “Schedule 80D should be blank if ‘New Tax Regime’ is selected.” | Confirms that under the **New Tax Regime** no Section 80D deduction (including health‑check‑up) can be claimed at all. |\n\n#### Interpretation\n\n1. **Old Tax Regime** – Section 80D allows two distinct heads of deduction:  \n   * **Health‑insurance premium** (up to ₹25,000 for self/family, ₹25,000 for parents, higher limits for senior citizens) – see C1‑C5.  \n   * **Preventive health‑check‑up** – capped at ₹5,000 (C1, C2, C5).  \n\n   The rules only **limit** the preventive‑check‑up amount; they do not require its presence. The dropdown logic in C6 explicitly lets a taxpayer indicate “No” for the check‑up and still claim the insurance‑premium portion.\n\n2. **New Tax Regime** –",
    "final_answer": "Under Section 80D, deduction up to Rs 25,000 is allowed for health insurance premiums. Preventive health check-up is allowed up to Rs 5,000 within the overall ceiling.",
